# The module's capstone is the one exercise here that must size auxiliary
# storage from the CALLER'S data -- which is why its `allowed` list is the only
# one besides create_node/insert_data to carry malloc and free. A breadth-first
# walk has to remember the nodes it has reached but not yet visited, and the
# cheapest place to put them is a fixed-size array.
#
# Nothing else in this repo can contradict that guess. Measured on a BFS with
# two 64-slot level buffers: tests/ex07/expected.txt reproduced BYTE FOR BYTE
# (17/17 PASS), clean under ASan+UBSan on the fixture's own shapes, the 400k
# differential OK and the 200k crash-fuzz OK. It has to be: the fixture's
# largest tree is 6 nodes and the corpus is capped twice over -- the oracle
# draws at most 10 items and diff_btree_apply_by_level.c declares
# `char *items[64]`. The valgrind layer is no help either, because the array
# that overflows is normally on the stack, which memcheck does not police.
#
# So the size of a tree is an axis no layer measures, and this probe measures
# it on both of its dimensions: a COMPLETE tree of depth 10 (1023 nodes, bottom
# level 512 wide) and a 200-deep SPINE, handed over with every node and every
# item malloc'd to its exact size. The two are not redundant -- measured, each
# catches a mutant the other misses. The complete tree reds the 64-slot level
# buffers and the spine does not (a spine is one node per level, so it overflows
# no queue). The spine reds a variant whose queue IS sized from the node count
# but whose level boundaries live in an `int level_end[64]` -- also byte-exact
# on the fixture -- and the complete tree does not, because it is only ten
# levels deep. Two correct shapes were run against both, a queue sized by
# counting first and a queue-free one recursive pass per level: clean, so the
# probe reds neither.
#
# It asserts nothing about the OUTPUT -- visit order, level numbers and
# is_first_elem belong to the *_output and *_diff layers, and a second red
# saying the same thing would only be noise. See the probe's own header for
# what each shape is aimed at.
c_mem_check(
    num = "07",
    fn = "btree_apply_by_level",
    probe = "mem_btree_apply_by_level.c",
    hdrs = ["deliverable/ex07/ft_btree.h"],
)


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ file set
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- cost
# Same unit, same reason, as c-12: every other c_cycles target measures per
# BYTE over a hex field, and this corpus encodes a STRUCTURE -- the insertion
# sequence a tree is built from, comma-joined. A per-byte figure over "61,62,63"
# would measure the corpus's decimal formatting, not the traversal.
#
# The unit is the NODE, counted off that field: commas plus one, except an empty
# field which is zero rather than one. gsub() returns the number of
# substitutions it made, so substituting a comma for a comma counts them.
#
# No instruction budget: a budget is a MEASURED number, and the only honest way
# to get one is to measure a correct implementation of this exercise, which does
# not exist yet. The per-node figure is reported meanwhile.
#
# syscall_budget = 0 is assertable from the subject and is the strongest thing
# these targets can say: a traversal applies a function, it does not print.
# btree_apply_* take the function to call as an argument, which is precisely the
# subject saying the printing is not theirs to do.
NODE = "($1==\"\"?0:gsub(/,/,\",\",$1)+1)"

